\begin{abstract}
We study data-free top-$k$ pruning of attention, in which a fixed budget of $k$ positions out of a context of length $L$ is retained, and connect two families of theorems to a five-rung ladder of measurements at model depth $d = 4$ and contexts $L = 128,\dots,2048$.

The first family concerns the \emph{threshold} (the knee): the least budget on a sweep grid at which retained accuracy reaches $0.98$ of the unpruned model's held-out accuracy. At $L = 2048$ the knee is $256 = dL/32$, confirming a prediction stated in advance and completing an exact chain of five doublings. We prove that this claim has an exact robustness radius: it survives every $\eta$-perturbation on the grid if and only if $\eta \le 0.0013$, the measured margin; and that at $\eta = 0.006$, the inter-seed spread of the same family, an admissible monotone curve reads one grid step lower. We introduce the \emph{certification depth} of a margin ladder, prove it unique and antitone in the noise, and compute it for the measured ladder: depth $5$ at $0.0013$, $4$ at $0.002$, $2$ at $0.004$ and at $0.006$, $0$ at $0.010$. A chain exact at five doublings is certified at two. We also show that the product law $k = dL/32$ is logically identical to context-independence of the deployable speedup $L/k = 32/d$, and that a five-rung exact chain has null probability $2^{-5} < 0.05$ under a one-grid-step coin model.

The second family concerns the attention profile. Double counting shows the random-$k$ control retains exactly $k/L$ of the mass; hence the selection gap is non-negative for every profile (its positivity is uninformative), and a vanishing gap at any intermediate budget forces exactly uniform attention. A Cauchy--Schwarz cap $T_k^2 \le k/\Neff$ yields a \emph{no bounded working set} theorem and, applied to the reported statistics, exposes an internal inconsistency, forcing the conclusion that the knee is a statement about the layer's output rather than about the attention distribution. Finally, an exchange argument proves that the top-mass functional is exactly invariant under self-similar refinement at matched sparsity, $T_{2k}(\split p) = T_k(p)$; therefore \emph{any} measured change of the selection gap across a context doubling refutes exact self-similarity of the profile. The observed decay from $+5.9$ to $+1.7$ accuracy points is such a change.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The cost problem and the budget response}

An attention layer of context length $L$ performs $\Theta(L^2)$ pairwise interactions. Restricting each query to a fixed budget of $k \le L$ key positions reduces this to $\Theta(kL)$, a speedup factor
\[
  \mathrm{speedup}(L,k) = \frac{L}{k}.
\]
\emph{Data-free} pruning fixes the retained set from the attention weights alone: keep the $k$ positions of largest weight.

Fix a bar $\beta$ (here $\beta = 0.98$) and a finite sweep grid $G \subset \mathbb{N}$. The \emph{knee} $k^{*}$ is the least $k \in G$ at which the pruned model's held-out accuracy reaches $\beta$ times the unpruned model's held-out accuracy.

\subsection{The product law and the ladder}

Across a series of controlled runs at depth $d$ the knee has repeatedly matched the \emph{product law}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pl}
  k^{*} = \frac{d\,L}{32}.
\end{equation}
At $d = 4$ this predicts $16, 32, 64, 128, 256$ at $L = 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048$. The present study measures the last of these---sixteen times the shortest context, five doublings, a single seed---and reads $k^{*} = 256$, as predicted in advance.

\subsection{Contributions}

\emph{Threshold register} (\S\ref{sec:knee}--\S\ref{sec:deploy}): the exact robustness radius of the final rung, the certification depth of the chain, the identity between the product law and a context-independent speedup, and the null probability of a five-rung coincidence.

\emph{Profile register} (\S\ref{sec:profile}--\S\ref{sec:exchange}): the exact random baseline, non-negativity and rigidity of the selection gap, a concentration cap with a no-bounded-working-set theorem and an internal consistency check, and the exchange theorem making the selection gap an exact invariant of self-similar refinement.

